│ patch_name               │ serial_trie_rowwise_reorder_fix                                                           │
│ patch_description        │ The previous implementation failed validation because its recursive value-grouping        │
│                          │ machinery was fundamentally broken: it relied on `hasattr(row, col)` attribute access on  │
│                          │ itertuples rows (fails/misses for many column names), wrote rows into `result_df` via     │
│                          │ `as_completed` (destroying row order and index alignment), manipulated a fabricated       │
│                          │ "original_index" column with fragile assertions, returned an empty `[]` as                │
│                          │ column_orderings (violating the required per-row orderings output), and contained         │
│                          │ assertion paths that crash when grouping/dependency assumptions don't hold (e.g.          │
│                          │ nunique==1 assertions, shape mismatches after recursive merging, duplicate index          │
│                          │ asserts).                                                                                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix replaces the whole editable block with a correct, bounded algorithm aligned with  │
│                          │ the actual objective (character-Trie prefix reuse): build each row's serialization from   │
│                          │ its cells, generate 3 cheap whole-df candidate constructions (original order, global      │
│                          │ frequency-weighted column order using sum len(v)*cnt*(cnt-1), and a bounded conditional   │
│                          │ partition tree), score each with the exact serial-Trie objective (sum of adjacent LCPs of │
│                          │ sorted serialized rows, using C-speed slice comparisons), and output the best with valid  │
│                          │ per-row column orderings. All cell values, rows and shape are preserved exactly (object   │
│                          │ dtype); col_merge is handled via the parent's merging_columns helper as before.           │
