│ patch_description        │ The previous program failed validation for several fundamental reasons: (1) it returned   │
│                          │ an empty list `[]` as `column_orderings`, which cannot describe the per-row column        │
│                          │ permutation it applied; (2) `recursive_reorder` built results with                        │
│                          │ `pd.DataFrame(...values.tolist() + ...)` concatenations and added a fabricated            │
│                          │ `original_index` column, altering row/column identity and dropping columns during merges; │
│                          │ (3) `column_recursion` iterated `as_completed` futures and wrote `result_df.loc` with an  │
│                          │ enumerate index, scrambling row order and leaving rows unassigned (DataFrame was created  │
│                          │ empty so `.loc` appends in completion order); (4) after `col_merge` it asserted           │
│                          │ inconsistent shapes and dropped the merged columns from the output entirely, changing     │
│                          │ shape relative to input; (5) multiple prints of internal state polluted stdout. The fix   │
│                          │ replaces the entire editable block with a correct, bounded algorithm: serialize cells     │
│                          │ once (missing → ""), factorize values to integer codes, build at most three cheap         │
│                          │ candidate per-row column orderings (a global frequency/length-ranked order, a conditional │
│                          │ partition tree using length-weighted pair repetition count*(count-1), and a               │
│                          │ length-then-frequency variant), score each candidate with the exact serial character-Trie │
│                          │ objective (sum of adjacent LCPs over sorted serialized rows, computed via C-speed prefix  │
│                          │ binary search), and return the best. Data and shape are fully preserved: every cell value │
│                          │ is output exactly once per row via the per-row permutation, merged-column groups and      │
│                          │ one-way dependencies are kept contiguous, and the returned `column_orderings` lists are   │
│                          │ exactly the permutations used.                                                            │
