│ patch_type               │ fix                                                                                       │
│ patch_name               │ trie_reuse_permutations                                                                   │
│ patch_description        │ The previous program was fundamentally broken: it returned an empty `column_orderings`    │
│                          │ list (API requires valid per-row orderings consistent with the returned data), it         │
│                          │ reconstructed DataFrames from raw `values.tolist()` concatenations (corrupting data by    │
│                          │ coercing mixed types and mixing row/column semantics), it added an `original_index`       │
│                          │ column and dropped it, and its "recursive" grouping shredded cell/column alignment (e.g., │
│                          │ `final_result_df.columns` assigned from a truncated list). Its greedy value-based         │
│                          │ recursion also targeted squared-field-length heuristics instead of the actual             │
│                          │ character-Trie objective, and it depended on fragile `itertuples` attribute access that   │
│                          │ fails for arbitrary column names.                                                         │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix replaces the whole editable block with a clean, correct implementation:           │
│                          │ 1. Serialize every cell exactly once (missing → "" for scoring only; stored values        │
│                          │ untouched).                                                                               │
│                          │ 2. Build at most three bounded candidate constructions: (a) a global frequency-ranked     │
│                          │ column order scored by `sum(len(v)*count*(count-1))`, (b) a depth-bounded conditional     │
│                          │ partition tree that picks a split column inside each group by length-weighted pair        │
│                          │ repetition on precomputed integer codes and assigns per-row suffix orders, (c) the        │
│                          │ original column order as a safe fallback.                                                 │
│                          │ 3. Score each candidate with the exact ideal Trie reuse (sum of                           │
│                          │ lexicographically-adjacent LCP lengths, computed with C-speed slice-equality binary       │
│                          │ search), pick the best, and return rows sorted by their serialized string.                │
│                          │ 4. Return the dataframe with original column names/values (identity preserved, only row   │
│                          │ order permuted) plus per-row permutations of the column names, so `row[orderings]`        │
│                          │ reproduces the intended serialization. `col_merge` is honored via the parent's            │
│                          │ `merging_columns` when available; `row_stop`/`col_stop` bound tree depth; `early_stop` is │
│                          │ the branching gain threshold; `distinct_value_threshold` excludes over-distinct columns   │
│                          │ from splitting.                                                                           │
