│ patch_name               │ prefix_trie_column_reorder                                                                │
│ patch_description        │ The original program was fundamentally broken: it fabricated an extra "original_index"    │
│                          │ column, shuffled/merged real data through a lossy recursion, returned an empty            │
│                          │ `column_orderings` list (invalid per-row orderings), and applied a `sort_values` that     │
│                          │ permuted rows and could crash on mixed-type data (the stdout shows it aborting partway    │
│                          │ through datasets). Root cause: the recursive grouping logic did not preserve shape/row    │
│                          │ identity and never produced valid output metadata.                                        │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Fix: replace the editable block with a bounded construction-selection algorithm aligned   │
│                          │ with the actual objective (character-Trie prefix reuse). It (1) serializes cells for      │
│                          │ scoring only (missing → "", values unchanged), (2) builds 2–3 cheap candidate global      │
│                          │ column orderings (length-weighted frequency ranking, count-based ranking, original order, │
│                          │ with col_merge groups kept contiguous and one_way_dep edges respected), (3) scores each   │
│                          │ candidate exactly by sorting the serialized rows and summing adjacent LCPs via C-speed    │
│                          │ binary-search slice comparisons, and (4) returns the best candidate's column permutation  │
│                          │ applied to a copy of the original dataframe with a valid per-row `column_orderings` list. │
│                          │ Data, shape, dtypes, and row identity are fully preserved; runtime is bounded and         │
│                          │ deterministic.                                                                            │
