╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ fix                                                                                       │
│ patch_name               │ trie_prefix_reuse_reorder_fix                                                             │
│ patch_description        │ The original program failed validation for multiple correctness reasons: (1) it returned  │
│                          │ an empty column_orderings list `[]`, which violates the required API contract that        │
│                          │ per-row column orderings must be valid and consistent with the returned data; (2) the     │
│                          │ recursive logic mutated cell placement across rows via `result_df.loc` indexed by         │
│                          │ completion order of futures (as_completed scrambles row indices), silently corrupting     │
│                          │ data; (3) the final global sort by all columns destroys any per-row ordering structure    │
│                          │ and can produce per-row column orders that are impossible; (4) it adds an                 │
│                          │ "original_index" column to the working frame and later drops it — if any other path       │
│                          │ fails, shapes mismatch; (5) heavy use of `Counter(df.stack())`, `getattr` on itertuples,  │
│                          │ and networkx makes it slow and fragile on mixed types (NaN handling in stack/unhashable   │
│                          │ cells crash).                                                                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix replaces the whole editable block with a clean, correct implementation that:      │
│                          │ - Serializes each cell exactly like the evaluator: `str(value)` with missing values as    │
│                          │ empty strings (only for scoring/serialization, never modifying stored values).            │
│                          │ - Builds a few cheap candidate constructions: (a) frequency-ranked global column order    │
│                          │ using sum(len(v)*count(v)*(count(v)-1)) with deterministic tie-breaks, (b) a conditional  │
│                          │ partition-tree ordering that picks, within each group, the remaining field maximizing     │
│                          │ length-weighted pair repetition (count*(count-1)) using precomputed integer               │
│                          │ factorizations (no pandas ops in inner loops), partitioning groups by that field's value  │
│                          │ codes and ordering suffixes per group, with bounded depth/group work and a deterministic  │
│                          │ cheap tail, and (c) a length-then-frequency alternative.                                  │
│                          │ - Scores each candidate exactly with the deterministic ideal-Trie objective: sort the     │
│                          │ serialized row strings and sum adjacent LCPs (computed via C-speed prefix-slice binary    │
│                          │ search, handling identical strings directly), then returns the best candidate's DataFrame │
│                          │ plus valid per-row column orderings.                                                      │
│                          │ - Preserves all rows, columns, and cell values exactly (object dtype, values copied       │
│                          │ verbatim), honors col_merge by keeping merged columns adjacent (merging columns into      │
│                          │ concatenated scoring position is not allowed to change stored values, so we only position │
│                          │ them adjacently), and handles NaN/mixed types safely.                                     │
│                          │ - Keeps runtime bounded: factorizations computed once per column, bincount used for       │
│                          │ counts, depth limits on the partition tree, and only ~3 whole-program candidates each     │
│                          │ scored once.                                                                              │
