│ patch_description        │ The failed program returned `[]` as `column_orderings` (violating the API which requires  │
│                          │ valid per-row column orderings), used heavy per-row DataFrame mutation/threading that     │
│                          │ broke shape guarantees, and printed debug output. Root cause: the recursive               │
│                          │ value-grouping machinery (`column_recursion`, `recursive_reorder`) produced dataframes    │
│                          │ with mismatched/lost columns and no consistent column ordering metadata.                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Fix: replace the editable body with a bounded, correct algorithm:                         │
│                          │ - Serialize each candidate column ordering exactly as the evaluator does (missing → "",   │
│                          │ astype(str)).                                                                             │
│                          │ - Build up to 3 cheap candidate global column orderings (length-weighted repetition       │
│                          │ `len*count*(count-1)` ranked, distinct-count ranked, original order).                     │
│                          │ - Score each candidate by the exact ideal character-Trie reuse (sum of adjacent LCPs of   │
│                          │ the sorted serialized strings, computed with C-speed binary-search slice comparison).     │
│                          │ - Return the best candidate: original data, original row order, and valid per-row column  │
│                          │ orderings. Honor col_merge by concatenating merged columns, preserving all values. No     │
│                          │ debug prints, no evaluator assumptions.                                                   │
