│ patch_name               │ rewrite_trie_lcp_candidates                                                               │
│ patch_description        │ The previous program was fundamentally broken for the actual objective: it used           │
│                          │ squared-length heuristics, heavy per-row `itertuples`/`getattr` column matching, thread   │
│                          │ pools over rows, `Counter(df.stack())` recomputation, and returned an empty               │
│                          │ column-orderings list with a DataFrame whose rows were not consistently permuted (also    │
│                          │ mutating/dropping columns via the "original_index" bookkeeping and `sort_values` on the   │
│                          │ materialized frame). It never produced valid per-row column orderings and could alter     │
│                          │ cell placement/serialization.                                                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix replaces the whole editable block with a clean, bounded implementation:           │
│                          │ 1. Serialize each cell once (`""` for missing, `str(v)` otherwise) into object arrays;    │
│                          │ factorize each column into integer codes with unique-value lengths precomputed.           │
│                          │ 2. Build three cheap candidate orderings: (A) global order ranked by Σ                    │
│                          │ len(v)·count·(count−1), (C) classic stats order (nunique asc, total chars desc), and (B)  │
│                          │ a conditional partition tree that, per group of rows, picks the remaining column with the │
│                          │ largest length-weighted pair repetition and partitions rows by its value, recursing with  │
│                          │ bounded depth.                                                                            │
│                          │ 3. Score each candidate exactly with the ideal serial-Trie objective: sort the per-row    │
│                          │ serialized strings and sum adjacent longest-common-prefix lengths (binary-search prefix   │
│                          │ comparison, all C-speed).                                                                 │
│                          │ 4. Materialize the winning per-row column permutation into an object-dtype output         │
│                          │ DataFrame (values copied verbatim, no coercion), return consistent per-row                │
│                          │ `column_orderings`, and keep `col_merge` honored via the base `merging_columns` when      │
│                          │ available. All rows/values preserved; runtime bounded (no pandas ops inside tree nodes,   │
│                          │ depth-limited branching).                                                                 │
