│ patch_type               │ fix                                                                                       │
│ patch_name               │ lcp_trie_candidate_reorder_fix                                                            │
│ patch_description        │ The previous program failed because its complex recursive value-grouping logic was        │
│                          │ broken: it added an "original_index" column, returned empty column_orderings (`return     │
│                          │ final_df, []` — no per-row orderings), relied on `hasattr(row, col)` attribute lookups on │
│                          │ itertuples that rarely match column names, mutated the input df, and produced             │
│                          │ shape/consistency mismatches (the final global sort also scrambled the per-row            │
│                          │ permutations it had built). None of that machinery reflected the actual objective:        │
│                          │ maximize character-Trie prefix reuse, which for a fixed set of serialized row strings     │
│                          │ equals the sum of adjacent LCP lengths after sorting, and is independent of row order.    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix replaces the broken recursion with a bounded candidate-selection approach:        │
│                          │ 1. Serialize each cell (NaN → ""), factorize each column to integer codes once.           │
│                          │ 2. Build three cheap whole-program candidates: (a) original column order, (b) a global    │
│                          │ frequency-ranked order scored by `len(v)*count*(count-1)` per column with deterministic   │
│                          │ ties, (c) a bounded conditional partition tree that recursively picks the field with the  │
│                          │ largest length-weighted pair repetition inside each row group and orders suffixes per     │
│                          │ group.                                                                                    │
│                          │ 3. Score each candidate with the true objective (sorted-string adjacent-LCP sum, using    │
│                          │ C-speed binary-search prefix comparisons), and return the best, materializing per-row     │
│                          │ column orderings and the row-permuted output df (same rows, same values, valid per-row    │
│                          │ orders, object dtype where mixed).                                                        │
│                          │ `one_way_dep` is honored by placing dependent columns adjacent to their parents;          │
│                          │ `col_merge` is delegated to the inherited `mererging_columns`. No data is added, removed, │
│                          │ or altered.                                                                               │
