├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ fix                                                                                       │
│ patch_name               │ rewrite_trie_lcp_candidate_selection                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ The failed program was deeply broken: it returned an empty `column_orderings` list        │
│                          │ (violating the required per-row orderings contract), relied on `getattr`/`hasattr` tricks │
│                          │ on `itertuples` rows that fail for non-identifier/space-containing column names (the      │
│                          │ stdout shows columns like `review/profileName` and                                        │
│                          │ `movieinfo_rottentomatoeslink_movietitle` going through the broken path), mutated/sorted  │
│                          │ rows via a fragile `original_index` merge, and never produced a consistent per-row        │
│                          │ permutation of the data. Its recursion (`column_recursion`,                               │
│                          │ `recursive_split_and_reorder`) also mixed row positions with `as_completed`               │
│                          │ (nondeterministic row assignment) and could drop/duplicate rows, failing shape/identity   │
│                          │ validation.                                                                               │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix replaces the whole editable block with a correct, bounded implementation that     │
│                          │ preserves every cell, row, and column:                                                    │
│                          │ - Serialize each column once (`fillna("")` then `astype(str)`) exactly matching the       │
│                          │ scoring representation.                                                                   │
│                          │ - Build up to three cheap candidate constructions: (1) a global frequency-ranked ordering │
│                          │ scored by `sum len(v)*cnt*(cnt-1)`, (2) a conditional partition tree that picks a leading │
│                          │ field per group by length-weighted pair repetition (integer codes, bounded                │
│                          │ depth/candidates) producing per-row orders, and (3) a length-weighted total-frequency     │
│                          │ ordering.                                                                                 │
│                          │ - Score each candidate with the true serial-Trie objective: sum of adjacent               │
│                          │ longest-common-prefix lengths over the sorted serialized rows (binary-search LCP so       │
│                          │ comparisons run in C).                                                                    │
│                          │ - Return the best candidate's data (per-row permuted values, object dtype, original row   │
│                          │ order preserved) plus valid per-row column orderings consistent with the returned rows.   │
│                          │ `col_merge` is honored with the existing parent `merging_columns` semantics; all other    │
│                          │ API parameters are accepted and used as bounded heuristics only.                          │
