│                          │ (its recursive machinery misused `itertuples` attribute access, dropped/renamed columns   │
│                          │ via "original_index", and returned an empty `column_orderings` list, so per-row orders    │
│                          │ were invalid). It also asserted shapes that failed once `col_merge` changed column        │
│                          │ counts, and its reward model (squared field lengths) doesn't match the actual             │
│                          │ character-prefix-Trie objective.                                                          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Fix: replace the editable block with a correct, bounded implementation. It (1) serializes │
│                          │ every row exactly like the evaluator (`"".join(fillna("").astype(str))`), (2) builds up   │
│                          │ to three cheap candidate orderings — a global frequency-ranked order (score = Σ           │
│                          │ len(v)·c·(c−1)), a shallow conditional-partition order that picks leading fields by pair  │
│                          │ repetition inside value groups, and a count-only variant — (3) scores each candidate with │
│                          │ the exact ideal Trie reuse (sum of adjacent LCPs over sorted serialized strings, computed │
│                          │ via C-speed binary search on prefix slices, capped by a total-character budget), and (4)  │
│                          │ returns the best candidate's DataFrame plus valid per-row column orderings. All cells,    │
│                          │ rows, and columns are preserved verbatim (only permuted per row), missing values and      │
│                          │ mixed types are handled via object dtype, and `col_merge` is honored via the base class   │
│                          │ `mererging_columns` when supplied.                                                        │
