│ patch_name               │ trie_prefix_caching_reorder_rewrite                                                       │
│ patch_description        │ The original program failed validation for multiple structural reasons: (1) it returned   │
│                          │ an empty list `[]` as `column_orderings` instead of valid per-row column orderings; (2)   │
│                          │ it mutated values/order via merges on `original_index`, dropped/merged columns, and its   │
│                          │ row-permutation recursion frequently dropped or duplicated rows (shape asserts /          │
│                          │ reset_index concat bugs, `result_df` built with mismatched columns, `itertuples`          │
│                          │ attribute lookups failing on spaces); (3) `col_merge` handling produced a different       │
│                          │ number of columns than the input; (4) heavy pandas per-row loops and recursion with       │
│                          │ Counters over `df.stack()` are extremely slow. The objective (exact character-Trie prefix │
│                          │ reuse on `"".join(row.fillna("").astype(str))` serialized rows) only requires per-row     │
│                          │ column permutations preserving all cell values.                                           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ I replaced the editable block with a bounded, correct algorithm: serialize cells once     │
│                          │ into object arrays, build 2–3 candidate per-row column orderings (global frequency-rank   │
│                          │ order weighted by len*count*(count-1); a recursive conditional partition tree on          │
│                          │ factorized integer codes with depth/size bounds and a deterministic frequency-ordered     │
│                          │ tail; per-row placement of long shared values near the front), compute the exact ideal    │
│                          │ Trie reuse via sorting serialized rows and a C-speed binary-search LCP sum, and return    │
│                          │ the best candidate. All rows/values preserved exactly (object dtype), per-row orderings   │
│                          │ returned, no evaluator state touched.                                                     │
