│ patch_name               │ rewrite_trie_prefix_reorder                                                               │
│ patch_description        │ The existing program failed validation: its recursive value-grouping logic builds         │
│                          │ DataFrames with mismatched/missing columns, uses positional tuple attribute hacks         │
│                          │ (`row._idx`) that break on real column names, mutates rows in place with                  │
│                          │ `result_df.update`, fabricates an `original_index` column that corrupts shape when        │
│                          │ `col_merge` is used, and returns `[]` as column orderings (invalid per-row orderings).    │
│                          │ The whole approach optimizes squared field-length group counts, not character-level Trie  │
│                          │ prefix reuse, and its assertions fail on mixed-type data (e.g., NaN in `stack()`-based    │
│                          │ Counters).                                                                                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ I replaced the editable block with a clean, correct implementation that targets the       │
│                          │ actual objective — character-Trie prefix reuse measured as the sum of adjacent LCPs over  │
│                          │ the sorted serialized rows:                                                               │
│                          │ - Serialize each cell once (missing -> "") into an integer-factorized per-column          │
│                          │ representation; count repetitions with vectorized bincount.                               │
│                          │ - Build three bounded candidate constructions: (A) global column ordering ranked by Σ     │
│                          │ len(v)·count·(count−1), (B) a length-then-repetition global ordering, (C) a conditional   │
│                          │ per-row grouping tree that picks, inside each row group, the remaining field with the     │
│                          │ largest length-weighted pair repetition and partitions rows by its value, with bounded    │
│                          │ depth and a deterministic cheap tail.                                                     │
│                          │ - Score each candidate exactly with the sorted-string adjacent-LCP sum (binary-search     │
│                          │ LCP, C-speed slicing) and return the best; per-row column orderings are returned          │
│                          │ consistently with the per-row permuted data.                                              │
│                          │ - All original cells, rows and shape are preserved (object dtype output); `col_merge`     │
│                          │ groups are concatenated into a single merged column; `one_way_dep`, `early_stop`,         │
│                          │ `row_stop`, `col_stop`, `distinct_value_threshold`, `parallel` are accepted per the API   │
│                          │ (row/col stops bound recursion depth, unused knobs are no-ops).                           │
