│ patch_type               │ fix                                                                                       │
│ patch_name               │ trie_reuse_row_orders_fix                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ The original program failed validation for several compounding reasons: (1) it returned   │
│                          │ an empty `column_orderings` list, which is not a valid per-row ordering consistent with   │
│                          │ the returned data; (2) it fabricated and later dropped an `original_index` column,        │
│                          │ mutating the data/shape mid-algorithm and relying on merges/asserts that can break on     │
│                          │ mixed-type data; (3) the recursive "group by max value" logic used thread pools,          │
│                          │ `df.isin(...).any(axis=1)` row splits and per-row attribute lookups that silently         │
│                          │ misplace cells and can drop or duplicate rows; (4) it optimized squared field lengths /   │
│                          │ exact-cell matches, which does not correspond to the character-Trie prefix-reuse          │
│                          │ objective.                                                                                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix replaces the broken machinery with a correct, bounded construction-selection      │
│                          │ algorithm: cells are serialized once (NaN/None -> "" per scoring rules), column groups    │
│                          │ from `col_merge` are kept as atomic adjacent blocks, and up to three cheap candidate      │
│                          │ row-ordering constructions are built — (a) original column order baseline, (b) a global   │
│                          │ frequency-ranked column order scored by sum(len(v)*count*(count-1)), and (c) a recursive  │
│                          │ conditional partition tree that picks, inside each row group, the remaining column with   │
│                          │ the largest length-weighted pair repetition and partitions rows by that column's          │
│                          │ serialized value (bounded depth, bounded candidates per node). Each candidate is scored   │
│                          │ with the real serial character-Trie objective (sort serialized rows, sum adjacent         │
│                          │ longest-common-prefix lengths via C-speed slice binary search), and the best candidate is │
│                          │ emitted. The output DataFrame preserves every cell value and the original shape (object   │
│                          │ dtype), with each row's values permuted according to its own column ordering, and         │
│                          │ `column_orderings` returns one valid per-row column-name list.                            │
