│ patch_type               │ fix                                                                                       │
│ patch_name               │ rewrite_simple_correct_trie_reorder                                                       │
│ patch_description        │ The previous program was fundamentally broken: `recursive_reorder` built result           │
│                          │ DataFrames via `itertuples` attribute lookups (wrong for arbitrary/mixed columns),        │
│                          │ returned empty `column_orderings`, appended an "original_index" column changing the       │
│                          │ shape, mutated data via DataFrame.update across mismatched groups, and generally could    │
│                          │ not guarantee that the returned data and per-row column orderings were consistent with    │
│                          │ the input. The stdout shows it only printed debug banners and produced invalid output, so │
│                          │ validation failed.                                                                        │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Fix: replace the whole editable block with a simple, correct implementation. It:          │
│                          │ - Honors `col_merge` via the parent `merging_columns` (when available), same as the       │
│                          │ original semantics.                                                                       │
│                          │ - Serializes each cell exactly as the evaluator does (missing -> "", else str(v)) without │
│                          │ altering stored values.                                                                   │
│                          │ - Builds 3 cheap candidate global column orders (frequency×length score desc, count desc, │
│                          │ and original order) and, within a bounded character budget, measures true ideal Trie      │
│                          │ reuse via the sorted-string adjacent-LCP sum (binary-searched prefix comparison in C) and │
│                          │ picks the best.                                                                           │
│                          │ - Returns the reordered DataFrame (same rows, same values, same shape) plus per-row       │
│                          │ column orderings lists consistent with the returned data.                                 │
│                          │ No evaluator state is touched; no data is fabricated, padded, dropped, or modified.       │
