│ patch_name               │ serial_trie_construction_selection_rewrite                                                │
│ patch_description        │ The previous program failed because it returned an empty `column_orderings` list (the     │
│                          │ evaluator requires valid per-row column orderings consistent with the returned data),     │
│                          │ mutated cell values via column merging and DataFrame re-slicing (violating data           │
│                          │ preservation), relied on `itertuples` attribute access that breaks on column names with   │
│                          │ spaces/slashes, contained failing assertions on shape after merging, and optimized a      │
│                          │ squared-field-length heuristic that does not match the true objective (character-level    │
│                          │ Trie prefix reuse over the serialized row strings).                                       │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix is a complete rewrite of the editable block that:                                 │
│                          │ 1. Preserves every cell value and the exact DataFrame shape (per-column object arrays, no │
│                          │ merging, no fabrication, no drops), returning `(reordered_df, column_orderings)` with     │
│                          │ per-row column permutations.                                                              │
│                          │ 2. Builds three bounded candidate constructions: (A) a global frequency-ranked column     │
│                          │ ordering scored by `sum len(v)*count*(count-1)`, (C) a different length/frequency         │
│                          │ tradeoff `sum len(v)*(count-1)`, and (B) a recursive conditional partition tree using     │
│                          │ integer factorization codes and vectorized bincount savings `len*c*(c-1)`, with depth     │
│                          │ bounds, candidate-column caps, and a deterministic global-order tail.                     │
│                          │ 3. Scores each candidate with the true objective — sum of adjacent LCPs over the sorted   │
│                          │ serialized row strings (`"".join` of `fillna("")` stringified cells), with binary-search  │
│                          │ LCP using C-speed prefix-slice equality — and returns the best candidate, so no           │
│                          │ per-instance regression is possible relative to any single heuristic.                     │
│                          │ 4. Honors `col_merge` by keeping merged columns contiguous as ordering "units" (without   │
│                          │ altering stored data), uses `early_stop` as the branching savings threshold,              │
│                          │ `col_stop`/`row_stop` as recursion bounds, and `distinct_value_threshold` to restrict     │
│                          │ branching candidates, while always including every column in every row's ordering.        │
