│ patch_type               │ fix                                                                                       │
│ patch_name               │ rewrite_serial_trie_reorder                                                               │
│ patch_description        │ The previous program was deeply broken: the recursive reorder used `getattr`-based        │
│                          │ attribute lookups on namedtuples that fail for arbitrary/duplicate column names, mixed up │
│                          │ row/column recursion with mutated `Counter` objects, asserted impossible invariants,      │
│                          │ constructed result frames with misaligned columns/index, and returned an empty            │
│                          │ `column_orderings` list while the returned DataFrame's cells were not permuted per-row at │
│                          │ all (plus it sorted rows and mutated `df` in place, adding an `original_index` column).   │
│                          │ Validation failed because the output was not a valid (data, per-row-orderings) pair.      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix replaces the whole editable block with a clean, self-contained implementation:    │
│                          │ 1. Serialize each cell exactly like the evaluator (NaN → "", else str) once per column;   │
│                          │ reuse across all candidates.                                                              │
│                          │ 2. Build at most three bounded candidate constructions: (a) original column order, (b) a  │
│                          │ global frequency-ranked order scored by Σ len(v)·count·(count−1), (c) a conditional       │
│                          │ partition tree that, within each group of rows, picks the remaining field with the        │
│                          │ largest length-weighted repetition gain (integer codes + per-node counting, bounded       │
│                          │ depth/candidates/nodes), giving per-row orderings.                                        │
│                          │ 3. Score each candidate with the exact serial character-Trie objective (sort serialized   │
│                          │ rows, sum adjacent LCPs, LCP computed with C-speed slice-equality binary search — no      │
│                          │ per-character Python loops), and return the best.                                         │
│                          │ 4. Emit the returned DataFrame by permuting each row's values according to its ordering   │
│                          │ (object dtype, original index/rows preserved), and return matching per-row                │
│                          │ `column_orderings`. `col_merge` is honored via the parent `mererging_columns`;            │
│                          │ `one_way_dep` is honored by placing dependent columns right after their source;           │
│                          │ `row_stop`/`col_stop`/`early_stop` bound the tree. No data is added, dropped, or altered. │
