│ patch_name               │ serial_trie_prefix_reorder_fix                                                            │
│ patch_description        │ The previous program was fundamentally broken: it mutated the DataFrame (added/dropped an │
│                          │ "original_index" column, dropped "too distinct" columns, merged columns and re-sorted     │
│                          │ rows), returned an empty column_orderings list (invalid — the API requires valid per-row  │
│                          │ orderings), used heavy per-cell attribute-access loops, and its recursion could lose or   │
│                          │ duplicate rows. Validation failed because the returned shape/data did not preserve the    │
│                          │ input and the orderings were unusable.                                                    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix is a complete rewrite that preserves every cell, row and column exactly:          │
│                          │ 1. Serialize each cell once (missing values -> ""), reusing the string matrix across all  │
│                          │ candidate constructions.                                                                  │
│                          │ 2. Build 2–3 bounded candidate orderings over column "units" (merged column groups kept   │
│                          │ adjacent per col_merge): (A) global frequency-ranked order using                          │
│                          │ sum(len(v)*count*(count-1)), (C) a length-weighted variant, and (B) a bounded conditional │
│                          │ partition tree that picks a leading column per group by length-weighted pair repetition   │
│                          │ using pre-factorized integer codes, with depth and candidate-column bounds.               │
│                          │ 3. Score each candidate with the true serial Trie objective (sort serialized rows, sum    │
│                          │ adjacent longest-common-prefix lengths via C-speed binary search on prefix slices) and    │
│                          │ return the best.                                                                          │
│                          │ 4. Return the DataFrame with original column labels, each row's values permuted according │
│                          │ to its per-row ordering, plus the per-row column-ordering lists (expanded from units),    │
│                          │ enforcing one_way_dep ordering constraints. Row identity and all stored values are        │
│                          │ unchanged; only column placement per row changes.                                         │
